WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.150
Nobody chooses to run DNS in five places.

2
00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:06.451
Each cloud ships its own DNS service,

3
00:00:06.451 --> 00:00:10.900
and none of them shares state with
Active Directory or with each other.

4
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:15.312
So the same name drifts into different
answers depending on where the query starts.

5
00:00:15.312 --> 00:00:19.421
That's split-brain, and it fails
quietly: the name still resolves,

6
00:00:19.421 --> 00:00:24.637
just to the wrong place, and it shows up as
a certificate error or an access denial.

7
00:00:24.637 --> 00:00:27.187
Runbooks can't keep five systems in step,

8
00:00:27.187 --> 00:00:31.900
and your real security posture is the
weakest resolver a client can reach.

9
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:34.483
So the decision for the sponsor and CIO:

10
00:00:34.483 --> 00:00:39.488
authorize one authoritative policy
plane, before the next migration wave.

11
00:00:39.488 --> 00:00:42.140
Next, what federal policy requires.
